From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 23:24:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: s2r: add the T8132 sleep sequence macOS
 uses, for bring-up

T8132 (M4) has no CPU complex sequence that is known to commit a
suspend-to-RAM, so deep sleep is refused there. Aurora traced macOS
sleeping on a J713 (T8132) and ported the sequence, which took 8 of 8 real
sleeps there. It has no CPU complex step and no runtime cluster gate.

Add that sequence to apple-s2r behind a new boot parameter,
apple_s2r.t8132_sequence, default off. With it off nothing changes: the
T8132 windows are not even mapped, and the preflight keeps refusing deep.

With it on, ->enter keeps this driver's own frame: the RVBAR check, the
coprocessors' refusals and stops, the enclave request, the breadcrumb, the
bring-up watchdog, abort_before and the warm boot mailbox. After that it
runs the T8132 order:

 - AP wake masks, all but apple,wake-enable;
 - macOS's power words before the commit: PMU boot stage 0x50, SPMI notify
   enables, and the words macOS has on, off and at state 4;
 - cpu_suspend(), then the parked CPUs stopped (apple_cpu);
 - ATC0's AON words lowered, WD2 armed for 5 s, SoC and DCS performance
   states, the memory controller stepped down, CPM gates and the fourth
   PMC voter, CPU cluster targets to 0, clock muxes parked, every power
   word saved, then the PMGR commit, the other cores' last WFI, 3 ms, and
   the boot CPU's own.

On the wake, entered through a stub that restarts WD2 for 60 s, it brings
back the clusters (E first, polled), the wake gates, every power word in
macOS's order, ATC0's words, the SEP's DEV_DISABLE cleared, the CPM gates
and WD2 for 120 s until ->end. Then the match is undone and the wake masks
are restored.

abort_before has four new steps for it: 12 wake masks, 13 power word match,
14 parked CPU stop, 15 IO lowering (the first SoC power-down write). 10
(CPU suspend) sits between the match and the stop. A real sleep is refused
for now while a display CPU or ANS's PCIe block is on: the engine waits for
the display CPUs with no timeout, and the APCIE lowering is not ported yet.
So only bring-up runs with abort_before can go ahead.

The register windows come from the device tree and are checked against the
J604's ADT. The 158 PMGR and 52 AON power words and every offset in the
tables are the same in the J604's and the J713's ADT, name for name.

Layouts, values and order follow Aurora's drivers/soc/apple/sleep.c in
aurora-silicon/linux#1314 (head 17161ffa69): apple_sleep_match(),
apple_sleep_match_undo(), sleep_wake_arm(), apple_sleep_arm_wd2(),
apple_sleep_dcs_down(), t8132_sleep_lower_io(), t8132_sleep_mcc_stop(),
t8132_clean_ram(), t8132_sleep_commit(), t8132_resume_stub,
apple_sleep_return(), sleep_ps_rewake() and the t8132_* tables. Its
authors are Bartosz Nitka and Diljit Singh, and the trace is aura#109.
Differences: the cores are stopped through apple_cpu's park protocol; a
core that does not take the stop gives the sleep up instead of committing;
the AIC is masked by its syscore suspend; and the IO lowering leaves APCIE
out (refused instead). Proven on the J604 only by bring-up cycles.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
